
What is HPA in Kubernetes?

HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler) in Kubernetes automatically scales the number of Pods (instances) in a Deployment, ReplicaSet, or StatefulSet based on metrics like CPU, memory, or custom metrics.

👉 In simple words:
HPA increases or decreases Pods automatically depending on the load on your application.



Based on metrics like:

	CPU (most common)

	Memory

	Custom metrics (Prometheus)

	🔹 1. Prerequisites ⚠


Why do we use HPA?


HPA is used to handle dynamic traffic/load efficiently.

🔹 Key reasons:

Automatic scaling

	When traffic increases → more Pods are created

	When traffic decreases → Pods are reduced

Better performance

	Prevents your application from slowing down during high load

Cost optimization

	You don’t keep unnecessary Pods running when traffic is low

High availability

	Ensures your app stays responsive even under heavy traffic


How it works 

HPA continuously monitors metrics (CPU, memory, etc.)

It compares current usage with the defined target

Based on that:
	If usage > target → scale up Pods

	If usage < target → scale down Pods


Example

	Suppose:

		You set CPU target = 50%

		Current CPU usage = 80%

		👉 HPA will increase the number of Pods

	If CPU drops to 20%

		👉 HPA will reduce Pods



